= Ban Lan Gen

#emph[Scope: This entry treats #emph[ban lan gen] (板藍根, bǎn lán gēn) — the canonical Chinese #emph[heat-clearing, toxin-resolving, throat-benefiting] drug-root of two distinct plant species: in #emph[modern Chinese Pharmacopoeia and northern Chinese commerce], #emph[Isatis indigotica] Fortune (= #emph[I. tinctoria] L., Brassicaceae — the same #emph[woad] plant of the European dyer's tradition and ancient British body-paint folklore); in #emph[southern Chinese tradition], #emph[Strobilanthes cusia] (Nees) Kuntze (= #emph[Baphicacanthus cusia], Acanthaceae). The two are #emph[botanically unrelated] but produce broadly similar #emph[indigo / indirubin]-class actives and have parallel clinical register; the modern Chinese Pharmacopoeia treats them as #emph[separate drugs] (北板藍根 #emph[běi bǎn lán gēn], northern; 南板藍根 #emph[nán bǎn lán gēn], southern) though regional clinical substitution is common. The #emph[same two plants] produce three distinct canonical Chinese drugs from #emph[different parts]: #emph[ban lan gen] (root); #emph[da qing ye] (大青葉, leaf); #emph[qing dai] (青黛, fermented-and-precipitated indigo pigment) — each is a #emph[separate canonical entry] in the Bencao with related but distinct profiles; this entry's primary subject is the #emph[root], with comparative notes on the leaf and the indigo. Status: classical in Chinese medicine (recorded under various names in the Tang–Song Bencao, formally established by the late-Ming and Qing); also #emph[classical in the Greco-Roman tradition] via the #emph[Isatis tinctoria / woad / glastum] dye-and-medicine register (Dioscorides, Pliny, Caesar on Britons); not classical in Ayurveda or Tibb. Modern register: the indirubin/qing dai → chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML) Chinese-medicine-to-Western-oncology translation is one of the cleaner classical→modern oncology bridges; the Chinese government's promotion of ban lan gen during SARS (2003), H1N1 (2009), and COVID-19 (2020) has generated substantial public attention with mixed clinical evidence.]

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* Two distinct species supply the canonical Chinese drug:
- *_Isatis indigotica_ Fortune* — Chinese woad; sometimes treated as conspecific with *_I. tinctoria_ L.* (the European woad) or as a distinct species/variety; family *Brassicaceae* (mustard family). The *root* is *board-shaped* (cylindrical, often longitudinally sliced for drying — the basis of the Chinese name 板藍根 "board-indigo root"). The same plant produces *da qing ye* (leaves) and is the principal modern source of *qing dai* (the fermented indigo pigment).
- *_Strobilanthes cusia_ (Nees) Kuntze* (= *_Baphicacanthus cusia_*) — Chinese Strobilanthes; family *Acanthaceae* (acanth family); the *southern Chinese, Vietnamese, and Taiwanese aboriginal* source of ban lan gen. The genus *Strobilanthes* contains ~350 species in Asian tropics; *S. cusia* is the principal medicinal/dye species.

The *botanical-family difference* is striking: Isatis is in *Brassicaceae* (with cabbage, mustard, broccoli) and Strobilanthes in *Acanthaceae* (with acanthus, justicia, ruellia). The two plants are *unrelated* but produce *similar indigo-class compounds* via *convergent chemical evolution* — both elaborate *indican* (the glycoside precursor) that on plant injury or fermentation releases *indoxyl*, which oxidizes to the *blue indigo dye* and to *indirubin* (the red isomer). The convergence is one of the more striking botanical-chemical parallels: two unrelated plants used interchangeably for centuries in Asian dyeing and medicine because they make the same colors via the same enzymatic pathway.#footnote[Botanical and chemical convergence between Isatis and Strobilanthes: standard botanical literature; the indican / indoxyl / indigo / indirubin pathway is well-characterized; phylogenetic distance between Brassicaceae and Acanthaceae is substantial. Modern Chinese Pharmacopoeia treats 北板藍根 (I. indigotica) and 南板藍根 (S. cusia) as separate drugs.]

*Chinese:*
- *板藍根 (bǎn lán gēn)* — "board indigo root": *板 bǎn* "board, plank" (for the root's typical sliced-board preparation) + *藍 lán* "indigo, blue, dyer's plant" + *根 gēn* "root." The canonical Chinese drug name.
- *北板藍根 (běi bǎn lán gēn)* — "northern board-indigo root": *Isatis indigotica*.
- *南板藍根 (nán bǎn lán gēn)* — "southern board-indigo root": *Strobilanthes cusia*.
- *大青葉 (dà qīng yè)* — "great green leaf": the *leaves* of the same plants (principally I. indigotica in modern pharmacopoeia); a separate Bencao drug with related but distinct clinical register (more emphasis on the surface, head, and skin manifestations of heat-toxin).
- *青黛 (qīng dài)* — "indigo / cyan-black": the *blue-black indigo pigment* prepared by fermenting the fresh leaves of either plant in lime-water until the indican releases and the resulting indigo precipitates; dried and powdered. Used internally and externally for heat-toxin in the blood, skin, and lung; modern Chinese clinical oncology uses *indigo naturalis* (the Western pharmaceutical name for purified qing dai) for *chronic myelogenous leukemia* (the indirubin → CML translation).
- *藍 (lán)* — the bare character: "indigo blue / indigo plant"; the source of many compound terms.

The *lán* character is one of the oldest plant-and-color words in Chinese — appearing in the *Shijing* (Classic of Poetry, ~10th–7th c. BCE) in agricultural and dye-plant contexts; the canonical Chinese sequence *青出於藍而勝於藍* ("blue comes from indigo and surpasses indigo") is a *Xunzi* (3rd c. BCE) proverb about students surpassing teachers, with the etymology of the dye-extraction tradition implicit.#footnote[Chinese etymology and classical references: _Hanyu Da Cidian_; _Shijing_ (Classic of Poetry); Xunzi, _Quanxue_ chapter. The Chinese dye-and-medicine tradition with Isatis/Strobilanthes is ancient.]

*Latin (the Greco-Roman tradition for Isatis):* *_Isatis_* — the Latinized Greek ἰσάτις (_isatis_, "woad"), used by Dioscorides. Latin synonyms: *glastum* — used by Pliny (_Naturalis Historia_ XXII.2) for the Celtic and British dye-plant; the same word as the source of the Welsh *glas* "blue/green." *vitrum* — Caesar's term in _De Bello Gallico_ V.14 for the British dye-and-body-paint plant ("All the Britons stain themselves with vitrum, which produces a bluish color"). The *vitrum* / *isatis* / *glastum* European register is the ancient classical Greco-Roman attestation of the same plant Chinese pharmacy uses as ban lan gen.

*English:* *Woad* — Old English _wād_, of West Germanic origin (Old High German _weit_, Dutch _wede_); the pre-modern European blue dye plant before *Indigofera tinctoria* (Indian indigo) became commercially dominant in the 17th c. The English *woad* and Chinese *ban lan gen* are *the same plant in different cultural-medical registers* — an unusual cross-system attestation rarely emphasized in either tradition.

*Sanskrit / Arabic:* *Not classical in Ayurveda or Tibb.* Isatis tinctoria has minor Central Asian use; Strobilanthes is Southeast Asian; the canonical Sanskrit / Arabic-Islamic pharmacopoeias do not include these plants. Modern Indian and Pakistani Unani has minor naturalized use (post-classical).

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*板藍根 (bǎn lán gēn)* is established in the canonical Chinese tradition; specific Bencao attestation: the herb appears in the *Tang Bencao* (新修本草, 659) and *Song-era* materia medica (notably *Tang Shenwei's _Zheng Lei Ben Cao_*, 1108); systematized fully in the *Bencao Gangmu*. The clinical register is *heat-toxin-resolving, blood-cooling, throat-benefiting* — fitting the broader *清熱解毒* (heat-clearing / toxin-resolving) drug class along with *金銀花* (honeysuckle), *連翹* (forsythia), *蒲公英* (dandelion).#footnote[板藍根 entries: _Tang Bencao_; _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Tang Shenwei, _Zheng Lei Ben Cao_; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cold (寒).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Bitter (苦 kǔ).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Stomach (胃).

*Actions & indications:*
- *清熱解毒 (qīng rè jiě dú)* — *clears heat, resolves toxin* — the cardinal action. For *epidemic febrile illness* (the classical *瘟疫 wēn yì* / *温病 wēn bìng* register — corresponding broadly to acute viral infections in modern terms), *erysipelas, mumps, scarlet fever, severe acute pharyngitis*. The herb's *清熱解毒* register makes it one of the principal Chinese drugs for *epidemic / acute infectious* heat-toxin patterns.
- *涼血利咽 (liáng xuè lì yān)* — *cools blood, benefits the throat* — for *severely swollen and painful throat* (especially the classical *爛喉痧* / scarlet fever rash with throat ulceration; the modern *acute streptococcal pharyngitis* and similar acute upper respiratory bacterial infections). The throat-benefiting register is the most clinically central modern use.
- *Epidemic / acute viral illness register (modern):* ban lan gen has become *one of the most widely-used Chinese over-the-counter herbal medicines* for *acute respiratory illness* (cold, flu) prophylaxis and treatment; the Chinese government promoted ban lan gen during *SARS (2003)*, *H1N1 swine flu (2009)*, and *COVID-19 (2020)* with substantial public attention. *Clinical evidence is mixed*: in vitro antiviral activity is documented against multiple respiratory viruses, but rigorous human-clinical evidence is modest and heterogeneous. Honest scholarly register: *robust traditional Chinese register, modest modern clinical evidence, substantial public-health-policy use during epidemics, with cautious modern Western evaluation*.
- *Mumps, parotitis (流行性腮腺炎)* — classical use for the acute swelling-and-fever of mumps; modern Chinese pediatric clinical use is substantial.
- *Hepatitis (modern register)* — ban lan gen is in some Chinese clinical protocols for acute viral hepatitis A and B; clinical evidence modest.

*Cautions:* Spleen-Stomach yang vacuity with cold pattern (the bitter-cold action is contraindicated in cold-vacuity patterns); chronic loose stools; the cold-bitter action is *not appropriate for chronic-vacuity conditions or for cold-pattern illnesses*. *Pregnancy* — caution at therapeutic doses.

The closely related drugs (same plants, different parts):
- *大青葉 (dà qīng yè) — leaves*: similar 清熱解毒 register but with stronger emphasis on the *surface, head, and skin* (rashes, erysipelas, acute eye and oral inflammation) — *more dispersing-and-clearing-the-surface* than the root.
- *青黛 (qīng dài) — fermented indigo*: bitter, salty, cold; Liver and Lung channels; *cools blood and resolves toxin in the blood and skin* — particularly for *skin disorders (psoriasis, eczema), epistaxis, hemoptysis, severe sore throat*. Used externally and internally; modern Chinese clinical practice uses qing dai (as *indigo naturalis*) for *ulcerative colitis* (a striking modern application with substantive clinical evidence in some Asian trials) and for *psoriasis* (with hepatotoxicity caveat — see Modern Pharmacology).

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Ban lan gen is not classical in Ayurveda._ The Indian classical pharmacopoeia uses *Indigofera tinctoria* (Indian indigo) — *nīlinī* (नीलिनी) in Sanskrit — as a related indigo-class drug, but Indigofera is a *legume (Fabaceae)* and is a distinct genus from both Isatis and Strobilanthes. The Indian *nīlinī* tradition is its own classical entry deserving separate treatment. *Isatis indigotica and Strobilanthes cusia are not in the Indian classical pharmacopoeia*; modern Indian integrative use of ban lan gen is post-classical Chinese-influenced extension.

Reading ban lan gen by extension on dravyaguna axes: tikta rasa, laghu-rūkṣa guṇa, śīta vīrya, kaṭu vipāka; pacifies Pitta and Kapha; aggravates Vāta in excess. Karma: _viṣaghna_ (toxin-resolving), _jvarahara_ (anti-fever), _raktastambhana_ (blood-cooling) — all by extension. *Post-classical extension only*, flagged.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Ban lan gen is not classical in Tibb._ The Chinese-medical drug is outside the canonical reach of the Avicennan tradition. Some Central Asian and Persian-region use of *Isatis tinctoria* as a dye plant is documented, but the medical-Tibb register is essentially absent. No Qurʾānic or Tibb al-Nabawī provenance.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *普濟消毒飲 (Pǔ Jì Xiāo Dú Yǐn, "Universal-Aid Toxin-Dispersing Decoction")* — *the canonical classical mumps and epidemic-toxin formula*; Li Dongyuan (12th c.); *板藍根* is constitutive, paired with 黃芩 (Scutellaria), 黃連 (Coptis), 玄參 (Scrophularia), 連翹 (Forsythia), 牛蒡子 (Burdock), 柴胡 (Bupleurum), 升麻 (Cimicifuga), and other heat-clearing surface-releasing herbs. *The historical context: Li Dongyuan developed this formula during a 1202 Hebei epidemic* (the *daitou wenyi* "big-head epidemic" — facial cellulitis or erysipelas with high fatality); the formula's success is one of the great historical victories of Chinese medicine over an epidemic outbreak. The formula is *continuously used in modern Chinese clinical practice* for mumps, severe pharyngitis with facial cellulitis, and related upper-body heat-toxin epidemic illness.
- *Modern ban lan gen-based preparations:* ban lan gen granules (板藍根顆粒) — the canonical Chinese over-the-counter herbal flu-and-cold preparation; available in pharmacy across China; widely consumed prophylactically during cold-and-flu seasons.
- *Compound formulas:* ban lan gen is in numerous modern compound preparations for acute respiratory illness; specific COVID-19 era preparations include *連花清瘟膠囊 (Lianhua Qingwen Jiaonang)*, which contains *大青葉* (the related leaf-drug) among its 13 ingredients — *not* ban lan gen specifically, but the closely related drug-class.
- *青黛 (qing dai) modern oncology and dermatology preparations* — purified *indigo naturalis* topical creams for *psoriasis* (substantial clinical evidence; the Taiwan- and mainland-China-developed *indigo naturalis ointment*); oral preparations for *ulcerative colitis* and *chronic myelogenous leukemia* (the indirubin register).

=== Greco-Roman (Isatis tinctoria / woad)

The European classical tradition treats *Isatis tinctoria* — the *same plant as Chinese 北板藍根* — but in a *quite different register*: principally as a *dye plant* (the pre-modern European source of blue), secondarily as a *medical drug* in the Greco-Roman canon.

- *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ II.184–185) treats *_isatis hēmeros_* (cultivated woad) and *_isatis agria_* (wild woad) separately; medical uses include *application to ulcers, wounds, and hemorrhage* (the astringent-and-cooling register; the cultivated form is preferred for medicine). *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XXII.2) gives extensive uses of *glastum* / *isatis* including wound-stanching, dyeing, and notes the Celtic use.
- *Caesar* (_De Bello Gallico_ V.14, 1st c. BCE): "*All the Britons stain themselves with vitrum [woad], which produces a bluish color and gives them a more terrifying appearance in battle*." The Boudicca and broader pre-Roman British body-paint tradition is the famous classical ethnographic register, though the precise extent of historical British woad-body-painting is contested.
- *Medieval European pharmacy*: woad persists as a wound-stanching, astringent topical drug; the *Tournai* and *Toulouse* (Languedoc) woad industries were major medieval European blue-dye economies; *replaced by Indigofera tinctoria* (Indian indigo, with much higher dye yield) from the 17th c. onwards, leading to economic collapse of the European woad industry.
- *Modern Western herbal*: woad has minor modern use; *not* an established modern phytotherapy drug; the *medical register effectively died in the 17th–18th c.* with the loss of the dye industry. The *Chinese ban lan gen register is the only major surviving medical tradition* using this plant family.

The *Greco-Roman → European woad → Chinese ban lan gen cross-system parallel* is one of the more striking pan-classical attestations of a single plant in *two largely independent medical traditions* (with substantively different clinical emphasis). The European tradition prioritizes the astringent-and-wound register; the Chinese tradition prioritizes the heat-clearing-and-toxin-resolving register. Both are *internally coherent* and *empirically grounded* — a useful cross-system observation about how different medical traditions can emphasize different facets of the same plant's profile.

=== Ayurveda, Tibb

No classical formulas.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The two plant sources occupy distinct climatic ranges:

- *Isatis indigotica / I. tinctoria (woad)* — *continental seasonal-extreme to warm-temperate Mediterranean*. The European woad belt (medieval Toulouse, Tournai) and the Chinese northern cultivation (Hebei, Henan, Anhui, Jiangsu) reflect the same broad climatic preference: cool winters, warm summers, modest rainfall. The plant is *biennial* — leaves in the first year, flower-and-seed in the second. Climatically apt for the continental seasonal-extreme zone.
- *Strobilanthes cusia* — *subtropical-humid to hot-humid tropical*. Native to southern China, Vietnam, Taiwan; the Taiwanese aboriginal *(Atayal, Truku)* indigo-dyeing tradition uses this species; Vietnamese northern hill-tribe (*Hmong, Tay*) indigo-dyeing also relies on it. Climatically suited to warm humid conditions.

See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (Northern Chinese ban lan gen)

The classical northern Chinese cultivation; cool winters allow root storage; warm humid summers allow vegetative growth. *Use register*: as the heat-clearing-toxin-resolving epidemic-illness drug, ban lan gen is climatically apt as a *seasonal preparation for the hot-summer epidemic window* and the *winter cold-and-flu season* — both periods of acute heat-toxin pattern manifestation in classical reading.

=== Hot-humid subtropical (Southern Chinese, Vietnamese, Taiwanese Strobilanthes)

The southern Chinese register. Strobilanthes is part of the southern Chinese damp-heat clearing pharmacy; the subtropical-humid climate generates the *damp-heat (湿热)* pattern for which the herb is canonical.

=== Modern use across climates

The modern Chinese over-the-counter ban lan gen granule register has spread worldwide via Chinese diaspora and global integrative medicine; modern use is climatically agnostic.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The classical Chinese clinical-pharmacy register places ban lan gen as a *seasonal acute-illness drug* (epidemic, summer-heat, winter cold-and-flu); modern prophylactic daily use (especially the Chinese government's encouragement during SARS / H1N1 / COVID) extends the classical register *beyond its traditional seasonal-and-acute context*. The classical principle is *use for acute heat-toxin, not chronically for vacuity or prevention*; the modern prophylactic register risks compounding Spleen-yang depletion with chronic daily intake. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (Isatis indigotica root):*
- *Indigo* and *indirubin* — the principal indigoid pigments; *indirubin* is the *red isomer*; both formed from the colorless precursor *indican* (an indoxyl glucoside) through enzymatic hydrolysis and oxidation. Indirubin is concentrated in *qing dai* (the fermented indigo pigment); the root contains trace amounts.
- *Tryptanthrin* — a yellow alkaloid; *anti-inflammatory and anti-microbial* in preclinical models.
- *Indolone* and other indole alkaloids — varying with species and processing.
- *Polysaccharides* — immunomodulatory in preclinical models.
- *Organic acids:* salicylic acid, ferulic acid; modest amounts.
- *Glucobrassicin* (in Isatis specifically, as a Brassicaceae) — a glucosinolate; degrades on cellular injury to indol-3-acetonitrile and related indole compounds.
- *Sterols and triterpenoids*.

The *indigo / indirubin* fraction is the most-studied pharmacological focus. *Qing dai* (青黛, indigo naturalis) contains *indirubin* at ~1–5% of dry weight — a relatively rich plant source — and is the basis of the *modern oncological and dermatological applications*.#footnote[Phytochemistry: extensive modern Chinese pharmacognosy; standard references in _Phytochemistry_, _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Planta Medica_; indirubin chemistry well-characterized.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antiviral (preclinical, mixed clinical)* — ban lan gen and related preparations show *in vitro activity against multiple respiratory viruses* (influenza A and B, respiratory syncytial virus, parainfluenza, *SARS-CoV-2 in some preclinical assays*); *in vivo and clinical translation is mixed* — preliminary trials suggest modest benefit for acute respiratory illness but high-quality RCTs are sparse and heterogeneous. The mechanisms are multiple — direct viral inhibition, immunomodulation, anti-inflammatory effects.
- *Indirubin / indigo naturalis / chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML)* — *the classical-to-modern translation story*: in 1980s China, *qing dai (indigo naturalis)* was clinically observed to induce remission in CML; the active was identified as *indirubin*; the mechanism is *inhibition of cyclin-dependent kinases (CDK1, CDK2, CDK5) and glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3β)*; the discovery led to *meisoindigo* (a semi-synthetic indirubin analog) and influenced the development of *modern CDK inhibitors* in oncology pharmacology. *The Chinese ban lan gen → qing dai → indirubin → CDK inhibitor lineage* is one of the cleaner classical-medicine-to-modern-pharmacology pathways in the materia medica.
- *Indigo naturalis for psoriasis* — *substantive Taiwan and mainland-China clinical evidence*; topical qing dai ointment for plaque psoriasis has *multiple RCTs* supporting efficacy comparable to standard topical therapy; *oral indigo naturalis for psoriasis is also used but with hepatotoxicity caveat* (see below).
- *Indigo naturalis for ulcerative colitis* — *a striking modern application*: a *2018 Japanese RCT* (Naganuma et al., _Gastroenterology_) demonstrated *significant clinical efficacy of oral indigo naturalis* in moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis, exceeding many conventional therapies. However, *pulmonary arterial hypertension* developed in some study patients as a *serious adverse effect*; subsequent safety analysis and clinical caution have moderated enthusiasm. Indigo naturalis for UC is a *modern Chinese-classical-medicine to modern-IBD-therapy translation* with both efficacy and safety dimensions.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — preclinical and modest clinical.
- *Hepatotoxicity (qing dai / indigo naturalis at chronic high oral dose)* — *documented case reports* of *hepatotoxicity, including severe and rare fatal cases*, with chronic oral high-dose qing dai used for psoriasis; the mechanism is unsettled (idiosyncratic vs. dose-dependent); *clinician supervision and liver-function monitoring* are appropriate.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Ban lan gen decoction:* 9–15 g per day; up to 30 g per day in acute serious infection per classical Chinese clinical practice.
- *Ban lan gen granules (modern Chinese OTC):* per product instruction, typically 5–10 g per day in 2–3 divided doses for cold/flu; not for chronic preventive use.
- *Da qing ye (leaf):* 9–15 g per day in decoction.
- *Qing dai (indigo):* 1.5–3 g per day in compound preparations; *indigo naturalis* for UC is dosed per clinical protocol (typically ~0.5–2 g per day under supervision).
- *Acute illness use:* typical course 3–7 days; *not for chronic daily prophylaxis*.
- *Cold-pattern conditions, Spleen-yang vacuity:* avoid.
- *Pregnancy:* avoid therapeutic doses.
- *Pediatric use:* in modern Chinese clinical practice, ban lan gen granules are commonly given to children for acute respiratory illness; pediatric dosing should follow product or clinician guidance.
- *Indigo naturalis (qing dai):* clinician supervision and *liver function monitoring* recommended for oral therapeutic use, particularly chronic dosing for psoriasis; *pulmonary arterial hypertension* monitoring relevant for ulcerative colitis dosing.
- *Drug interactions:* CYP3A4 modulation modest; not clinically significant at typical doses.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.* Qing dai therapeutic-dose use should be clinician-supervised given the documented adverse events.#footnote[Dose ranges and clinical pharmacology: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; modern Chinese clinical literature; clinical UC literature on indigo naturalis (Naganuma et al., _Gastroenterology_ 2018; subsequent safety analyses); psoriasis indigo naturalis literature (Lin et al. Taiwan studies); CML / indirubin literature (Chinese 1980s clinical observations; modern CDK inhibitor development); hepatotoxicity case literature for qing dai. Verify against current pharmacology and clinical guidelines.]

